  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Allabaksha Moulana Shaikh vs The State Of Maharashtra on 12 October, 2020 Author: C.V. Bhadang Bench: C.V. Bhadang 22 Aba st 1825-20 In The High Court Of Judicature At Bombay Criminal Appellate Jurisdiction Criminal Anticipatory Bail Application (Stamp) No. 1825 Of 2020 Allabaksha Moulana Shaikh .. Applicant V/s. The State of Maharashtra ..Respondent ---- Mr. Sachin Deokar for the Applicant. Mr. Y. M. Nakhwa, App for the Respondent-State. ---- Coram : C.V. Bhadang, J.",
    "facts": "Today, during the course of hearing, it transpires that except the offence under Section 328 of IPC, other offences namely under Section 188, 272 and 273 of IPC are bailable. Even the offence under Section 59 of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 ('the said Act' for short) which carries a maximum punishment of imprisonment of six months, going by Part II of Schedule annexed Cr.P.C., would be a bailable offence. It is not pointed out from any of the provisions contained in the said Act, Mamta Kale page 1 of 2 22 ABA st 1825-20 that the offence under Section 59 has been made non bailable. Coming to Section 328 of the IPC, prima facie, there are no allegations that the applicant had administered any such stupefying or unwholesome drug or any other thing with intent to cause hurt to any person.",
